Cyber Threat Intelligence is the collection and contextualization of data. 
This means collecting data about indicators, techniques, and tactics in order to perform informed, risk-based threat detection, mitigation, analysis, and response. 
In its simplest form, threat intelligence can look like a list of indicators of compromise, or objects that have been linked to known malicious activity.

we can use this type of tactical information to identify or confirm active threats in our environment. 
Threat Intelligence can also inform on tactics, techniques, and procedures that we can use to guide our daily operations.
For example: how do our engineers configure alerts? How do we approach investigations? What about threat response activities?

Or it may inform the organization on general policies for our teams to follow. At the highest level, strategic threat intelligence gives us and our leaders a wider understanding of
the current threat landscape so we can drive our overall organizational strategy.

Tactical intelligence includes Indicators of Compromise like URLs, IP addresses, file hashes, virus signatures, and other similar objects that have been linked to known threats.
Operational intelligence takes us to the top of the Pyramid. This intelligence not only helps improve monitoring and investigations, but it can also help drive system and policy decisions based on known attack techniques

