 Using Threat Intelligence

What is a File Hash

A file hash is a fixed-size string of characters that is generated by running the contents of a file through a mathematical algorithm. 
Creating a hash value from a file can help ensure that the file has not been corrupted or modified in transit. 
Hash values are also useful to identify malicious files in our network by comparing the hash of a suspicious file against already known malicious ones.
Changing a single bit in a file will result in a different hash value, that's why these types of IOCs are at the bottom of the Pyramid of Pain. However, it can be a low-effort test and a matching result can save a lot of time during an investigation. 


Different Types of Hashing Algorithms

There are multiple types of hashing algorithms, but the most common are Message Digest 5 (MD5) and Secure Hashing Algorithm (SHA) 1 to 3.
One of the main distinctions between these hashes is the length of the key used in the function: from 128 bits for MD5, 160 bits for SHA1, and up to 512 bits with SHA2 or SHA3. 

An MD5 hash can be compiled faster than a SHA one but provides less security or, in other words, can be "cracked easily" compared to SHA. 
For this reason, MD5s are most commonly used to validate data integrity, like making sure that a file wasn't modified in transit; while SHA is also used in digital signatures, message authentication codes, and other security applications and protocols, including TLS, SSL, and IPSec.

